ERD Design Rationale and justification

The Entity Relationship Diagram (ERD) for the Mobile Money sms processing system was designed to translate raw xml data into a scalable, normalized relational schema that guarantees data integrity and optimal query performance. The schema is organized around four core entities and a junction entity to handle complex transaction patterns.
Core Entities and cardinality 
Users (Sender and receiver): stores unique customer profile information(Phone number, name account status). Because a user can initialise or receive multiple transactions over time, a 1:M(One to Many) relationship exists between Users and Transactions.
Transaction_categories: Classifies financial movements into distinct operational types(e.g Bank Transfer, cash Out, Airtime Top up, Utility payment). Each category can encompass many transactions(1:M), allowing easy aggregation for analytics.
System logs: Tracks data extraction, xml parsing status, dead letter snippet routing. And processing with Transaction to provide full auditability for every processed record.

Resolving Many to Many Relationships 
A user can hold multiple roles across different transactions that can involve multiple parties (sender and receiver). To resolve potential M:N ambiguity, a junction/associative table named Transaction_Parties was introduced. This table links Users to Transactions via composite Foreign keys while specifying the exact participant role(Sender vs receiver) for each record.
Data integrity and constraints
Primary keys(e.g: transaction_id, user_id) utilize auto incrementing integers or UUID’S, while Foreign Key constraints enforce referential integrity 	across related tables. Check constraints prevent negative monetary amounts(amount >= 0), and standard Datetime fields ensure ISO-8601 compliance across all logged system events.
